WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Thanks for tuning in to SpaceX Daily, episode 28.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:06.860
It's July 10, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.680
From Starbase to Orbit, here's your SpaceX rundown.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:16.300
Booster 20s roll out to Pad 2 and the 420-ton crane installation

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:18.920
at Cape Gigabay marked concrete steps

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.960
toward higher Starship flight cadence

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.260
and ground operations.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.300
Teams rolled Booster 20 out for testing on Pad 2

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.840
ahead of the integrated flight test campaign.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.220
The hardware move continues the established sequence

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.860
of positioning boosters for static fire checks

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.440
and pre-flight preparations.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.500
This step aligns with the ongoing Starship program arc

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.000
that last saw flight 12 hardware flow

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.120
and now advances the open questions

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.300
of rapid reuse and orbital refueling timelines.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.940
The action keeps flight 13 preparations visible

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.480
on the test manifest while engineers

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.020
monitor catch hardware integration status.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:00.860
Observers can next track static fire telemetry releases

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.200
and any updates on tower compatibility.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.480
Installation of the 420-ton crane

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.700
at the Cape Gigabay facility enables direct handling

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:12.500
of Starship vehicles and super heavy boosters

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:14.520
during stacking and repositioning.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.020
The equipment supports braking over and moving stages

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.060
between storage, integration, and pad transfer

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.860
without relying solely on Starbase infrastructure.

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.120
This addition expands operational flexibility

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:30.280
for the Starship program by creating parallel ground

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.420
processing capacity at a second coastal site.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.180
Integration testing with actual hardware

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.460
will confirm load ratings and alignment

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.960
with transporter systems.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.700
The crane directly addresses the reuse cadence constraint

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:45.840
by shortening vehicle cycle times.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.300
A new Starship configuration completed its test campaign

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.700
and met the targeted performance objectives.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.660
The results validate structural and thermal design updates

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.520
incorporated ahead of the next flight test window.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:02.000
Data from the test feeds the iterative hardware loop

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.420
that targets full booster and ship reuse

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.880
plus orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.860
Engineers will now review telemetry to adjust parameters

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.300
for subsequent builds.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.780
The outcome maintains momentum on the flight test campaign

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.280
that began with earlier integrated missions.

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.520
NASA assigned the Roman Space Telescope

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.640
to a Falcon Heavy launch in August

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.880
for exoplanet searches and dark energy studies.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:29.920
The heavy lift vehicle provides the payload mass

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.840
and volume margin demonstrated

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.780
on earlier national security and science missions.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.060
This assignment continues Falcon Heavy's role

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.180
for observatory class payloads

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.820
that exceed Falcon 9 performance envelopes.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.060
Processing schedules at the Cape

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:47.020
will determine final launch readiness.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.360
The mission adds another high-value science flight

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.640
to the Falcon manifest.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.720
A Falcon 9 will deploy 24 Starlink satellites

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:57.740
from Vandenberg Space Force Base,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.460
extending the constellation's coverage footprint.

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.140
The mission sustains the record reuse cadence

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.520
on the Falcon fleet while Starlink scales direct to cell

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.100
and broadband capacity.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.000
Booster flight counts and turnaround intervals

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.900
will be measured against the annual launch rate ceiling

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.560
tracked in prior episodes.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.800
The Vandenberg site continues to support polar

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:22.100
and high-inclinations Starlink planes.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.000
Launch timing remains subject to range

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:26.100
and weather constraints.

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.840
SpaceX placed more Starlink satellites into orbit

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.360
during the first half of 2026

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.100
than in any previous six-month period.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.840
Sustained Falcon 9 flight cadence

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.060
and the opening of additional launch sites

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:41.080
drove the increase.

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:44.720
The record directly expands on orbit capacity

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.400
that supports subscriber growth

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.460
and direct to cell service questions.

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:51.600
Future quarters will reveal whether manufacturing

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.620
and regulatory limits allow the pace to continue.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.520
The deployment total reinforces Starlink's contribution

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:00.580
to overall constellation density.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.300
Icebase secured ride-share lunar delivery slots

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.500
aboard Starship for cargo missions

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.540
ahead of dedicated SpaceX lunar operations.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.960
The contract places icebase payloads

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:14.780
on early Starship lunar flights

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:16.220
while the vehicle maturers

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.500
toward operational lunar capability.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:21.940
The arrangement tests shared transport economics

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.320
before Starship lunar cadence scales.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.620
Payload integration schedules will set the timeline

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:29.420
for the first flight opportunity.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.980
This deal builds on prior eSpace announcements

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:34.740
about lunar freight services.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.600
The Japanese startup Elevation Space

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.380
is signing customers for its reentry capsule

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:43.420
while tracking SpaceX commercial return developments.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.900
The vehicle targets independent reentry services

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.600
outside Dragon's crew and cargo manifest.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.980
Dragon's proven reentry performance continues

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.780
to define technical benchmarks for new entrants.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:59.680
First mission timelines depend on regulatory approvals

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.200
and hardware qualification.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.360
The effort illustrates growing commercial interest

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.640
in return capability.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.780
One thing worth watching is that environmental

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.200
and scientific groups filed comments

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.600
urging the FCC to pause review

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.780
of orbital data center constellation applications.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.980
Siting insufficient oversight of mega constellation impacts.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.160
The groups argue that moving compute hardware

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.140
to orbit increases collision and spectrum risks

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:29.060
before adequate rules exist.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.800
Resolution would require the FCC

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.800
to complete a broader rulemaking

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.020
on large LEO systems before granting new authorizations.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:42.820
Observers on our SpaceX noted booster 20s rapid movement

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.860
to the pad after earlier testing

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.720
with some users updating prior predictions

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:48.940
about rollout timing.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:52.700
Photos of Thursday's Falcon 9 ascent from Florida

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.480
showed the characteristic jellyfish plume pattern

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:57.620
created by exhaust interacting

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.620
with upper atmosphere ice crystals.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.720
Users tracked the environmental petition

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:06.260
to the FCC regarding orbital data center applications,

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.420
highlighting concerns over spectrum and debris

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.080
from large constellations.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.960
On the AI front, Elon Musk stated that SpaceX

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.240
could exceed Earth's total value if its goals are met,

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.220
linking the comment to the first public reveal

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.280
of an AI satellite concept.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:27.020
The architecture places processing nodes in orbit

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:30.160
with data routed over laser intersatellite links

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.000
already proven on Starlink.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.820
Environmental groups separately called

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.480
for a halt to orbital data center applications,

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.260
arguing that compute constellations

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.440
require the same regulatory scrutiny

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.700
already applied to broadband mega constellations.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:48.160
From an engineering standpoint,

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:52.240
the 420 ton crane now installed at Cape Gigabay

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.100
addresses a fundamental constraint

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.040
in starship ground operations.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.160
How to move and orient two massive integrated stages

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.320
without dedicated heavy lift hardware at every site.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.720
Raw structural steel and hydraulic capacity

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.440
set a floor cost measured in tons of material

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:11.540
and kilowatt hours of fabrication.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:14.660
The finished crane multiplies that cost

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:16.720
by the need for precision positioning,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:18.840
safety margins and integration

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:21.000
with existing transporter systems.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.640
By collapsing the number of handling steps

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.880
required between storage, stacking and pad transfer,

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:30.980
the crane reduces both time and risk per vehicle cycle,

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.280
directly attacking the reuse cadence bottleneck

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:36.200
that still limits annual flight rate.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.580
The design choice also reflects

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.880
a first principles trade off between site specialization

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.380
and fleet scalability.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.560
A single crane sized for the full booster

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.940
in plus ship stack avoids the need

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:51.720
for multiple smaller lifts or custom fixtures,

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:54.860
lowering the idiot index of ground operations

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.300
relative to a more fragmented approach.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.340
This mirrors earlier decisions at starbase

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.220
where tower and transporter geometry were sized

00:08:03.220 --> 00:08:05.840
to the vehicle rather than forcing the vehicle

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.380
to fit legacy equipment.

00:08:08.200 --> 00:08:11.240
Parallel site capability further distributes thermal

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:14.240
and structural loads across two coastal facilities,

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.120
reducing single point weather or maintenance risks

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.520
that previously constrained launch tempo.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.820
What the crane unlocks next is the ability

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:26.580
to sustain parallel processing flows at two coastal sites,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:29.580
which in turn supports the higher launch tempo

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.280
required for orbital refueling demonstrations

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:34.740
and sustained starlink replenishment.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:37.480
Load testing sequences will reveal

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:40.460
actual cycle times versus theoretical projections

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:43.780
and any deviation will highlight remaining friction

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:45.800
in transporter crane interfaces.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.120
Listeners should watch how quickly

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.820
the crane completes load testing

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:51.720
and enters routine operations

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:53.880
because each successful cycle

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:56.280
trims the marginal cost of the next flight.

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.760
The same logic that drove Raptor engine iteration,

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.420
reducing the gap between raw material cost

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:03.760
and delivered performance

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.040
now applies to ground systems

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.020
where every saved handling step

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:10.620
compounds across dozens of annual flights.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:16.380
And a quick market note, SPC X is at $152.16

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.820
down 0.1%.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:21.880
Before we go, watch for load test results

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.500
on the new Cape Crane and any telemetry

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.860
from booster 20s upcoming static fire.

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.900
That's the day at SpaceX.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:30.100
That's a wrap.

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:34.860
SPC X is trading at $152.16

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:37.520
down 0.1%.

